===Etymology===
{{PIE root|gem-pro|h₁lewdʰ}}
(Closely related to [[leode|lēode]] and [[leodan|lēodan]].) From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{m|gem-pro|*liudiz}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*h₁lewdʰ-||men, people}}. Cognates include Old High German [[liut]], Old Norse ''ljōðr''; and, outside the Germanic languages, Lithuanian [[liaudis|liáudis]] ‘common people’, Old Church Slavonic ''l’udъ'' (Russian [[люд]]).

